CAT = "trcnSafeSchools"; SEG = "SAF"
# Safeguarding: Prevention and Awareness (26.3%) and Professional Ethics and Conduct (15.7%).
Q = [
("What is the main purpose of holding regular fire and evacuation drills?",
 "So everyone knows what to do", ["To end lessons early", "To test the fire alarm bell only", "To punish late pupils"],
 "Drills make the evacuation route and assembly point familiar, so pupils and staff act quickly and calmly in a real emergency."),
("Which practice builds a safe school culture?",
 "Clear ways for pupils to report", ["Ignoring minor bullying", "Teachers working alone in secret", "Locking all exits by day"],
 "A safe culture lets pupils raise concerns easily and knows they will be acted on. Locked exits and ignored bullying increase risk."),
("Nigeria's school safety framework stresses safety, security and violence-free schools. Which action best fits it?",
 "Training staff for emergencies", ["Leaving gates open all day", "Allowing visitors anywhere", "Keeping no emergency phone contacts"],
 "Training staff, controlling access, and planning for emergencies are core parts of making schools safe, secure and free from violence."),
("Teaching pupils the difference between safe and unsafe touch and whom to tell is an example of:",
 "Personal safety education", ["Physical education", "Moral punishment", "Career guidance"],
 "Age-appropriate personal safety lessons help children recognise abuse and know where to get help, a key preventive measure."),
("A school safeguarding action plan should include:",
 "Roles, procedures and drills", ["Only the sports calendar", "Only the fee schedule", "Only exam timetables"],
 "A safeguarding and safety plan sets out who does what, reporting procedures, risk assessments and evacuation drills."),
("Why should a school involve parents and community leaders in safety planning?",
 "Safety extends beyond the gate", ["To reduce staff numbers", "To avoid writing policies", "To shorten the school day"],
 "Many risks, such as unsafe routes to school, lie in the community, so collaboration with parents and leaders makes children safer."),
("Which is the most appropriate way to discipline a pupil who disrupts the class?",
 "A calm, fair consequence", ["Public humiliation", "Hitting with a cane", "Denying him food"],
 "Non-violent, fair consequences correct behaviour without harming the child. Humiliation and physical punishment are harmful and violate safeguarding principles."),
("A teacher who has finished a safeguarding course should next:",
 "Apply it and refresh it", ["Never think of it again", "Keep the content secret", "Only train new pupils"],
 "Safeguarding training needs to be put into practice and refreshed, as procedures and risks change."),
("Which behaviour breaches professional boundaries between a teacher and a pupil?",
 "Private chats on social media", ["Praising good work in front of the class", "Marking homework promptly", "Meeting parents at school"],
 "Private one-to-one messaging with a pupil on social media crosses professional boundaries. Contact should be open, professional and through school channels."),
("A teacher receives money from a parent to give her child higher marks. Under the TRCN Code of Conduct this is:",
 "Professional misconduct", ["A normal gift", "Acceptable if small", "A private matter"],
 "Accepting inducements to alter results breaks the integrity required by the TRCN Code of Conduct and is professional misconduct."),
("Under the TRCN Act, allegations of professional misconduct against a registered teacher are first looked into by the:",
 "Teachers Investigating Panel", ["Parents-Teachers Association", "School debating club", "State Electoral Commission"],
 "Section 9 of the TRCN Act sets up a Teachers Investigating Panel in each state and the FCT to look into allegations, and refers cases with a case to answer to the Teachers Disciplinary Committee, the tribunal often called the Teachers Tribunal."),
("Under the TRCN Act, which tribunal decides cases of professional misconduct and can order a teacher's name struck off the register?",
 "The Teachers Disciplinary Committee", ["The Federal High Court", "The school PTA", "The NUT executive"],
 "Section 9(1) of the TRCN Act establishes a tribunal known as the Teachers Disciplinary Committee (often called the Teachers Tribunal). Under section 10 it can reprimand a teacher or order the registrar to strike the name off the register; appeals go to the Court of Appeal."),
("A teacher sees a colleague regularly keep one pupil alone in a locked classroom after school. She should:",
 "Report it to the safeguarding lead", ["Mind her own business", "Ask the pupil to keep quiet", "Post about it online"],
 "Concerns about a colleague's conduct must be reported through the school's procedure, just like any other safeguarding concern."),
("Which practice keeps the school compound physically safe?",
 "Repairing broken railings", ["Storing chemicals in classrooms", "Letting pupils climb old walls", "Using cracked buildings"],
 "Minimum safety standards include safe buildings, secure fencing and safe storage of hazardous materials; hazards should be fixed promptly."),
("Which statement reflects the teacher's duty of care?",
 "Act as a careful parent would", ["Only teach the syllabus", "Care only during lessons", "Leave all safety to the gateman"],
 "Teachers stand in loco parentis, acting as a careful parent would for pupils in their charge, throughout school activities."),
("Which practice best reduces bullying in a school?",
 "A clear anti-bullying policy", ["Telling victims to fight back", "Ignoring it as normal", "Moving the victim away"],
 "A clear policy, known to pupils and staff, with reporting routes and consistent responses, prevents and reduces bullying."),
("A teacher posts a photo of a pupil's poor test script online as a joke. This mainly breaches:",
 "The pupil's dignity and privacy", ["The school's dress code", "The fire safety plan", "The scheme of work"],
 "Sharing a pupil's work or image to mock them violates their dignity and privacy and breaches the TRCN Code of Conduct."),
("During an emergency evacuation, the class teacher's key task at the assembly point is to:",
 "Take a roll call of the class", ["Start the next lesson", "Go back for her bag", "Send pupils home"],
 "A roll call at the assembly point confirms every pupil is out, so anyone missing can be reported at once."),
("Which is a sign of a healthy professional relationship with learners?",
 "Fair treatment of every pupil", ["Favouring relatives and friends in class", "Giving gifts to some pupils", "Sharing personal problems"],
 "Professional relationships rest on fairness, respect and clear boundaries. Favouritism and gifts can create pressure and suspicion."),
("A visitor walks into a classroom without signing in at the gate. The teacher should:",
 "Direct him to sign in", ["Let him address the class", "Leave the class with him", "Ignore him completely"],
 "Visitor control, signing in and wearing a badge, is a basic school security measure that protects pupils."),
]
